\section{Introduction and usage}
\label{tex:Intro}

The aim of this include file is to make building LaTeX documents easier.
First we want to add suffix rules for LaTeX similar to those already in 
make(1)~\cite[see][Sect.\ 10.2]{GNUMake} for \eg (plain) TeX and C.

The main goal is to improve the default rules of GNU make; to change from plain 
TeX to LaTeX, to change from DVI-format to PDF-format.
We provide several suffix rules:
first, for ordinary documents, \ie to compile a [[.tex]] file to [[.pdf]],
but we also provide suffix rules for [[.dvi]];
second, for classes and packages, \ie to compile
\begin{enumerate*}
\item a DocTeX [[.dtx]] file to [[.pdf]] or [[.dvi]] and
\item an [[.ins]] file to [[.cls]] or [[.sty]].
\end{enumerate*}
The suffix rules we provide here follows the conventions set out 
in~\cite[Sect.\ 10.2]{GNUMake}.

To do the main LaTeX compilation, we use the following set up.
We note that [[xelatex]] requires the [[-8bit]] flag to be able to handle tabs 
in [[minted]] output.
Although, the option [[-halt-on-error]] seems interesting to stop the 
compilation if an error occurs, so that we don't miss it, it doesn't work.
The reason is that [[latexmk]] doesn't stop on error, it just keeps on going.
\TeX{} on the other hand stops that round of execution, but doesn't ask for 
input---which it does otherwise.
So the [[-halt-on-error]] option actually makes it more likely that we miss
that an error occurred.

After compiling we rerun LaTeX while the log still asks us to, but only up to
five extra times.
The bound is what makes the loop terminate at all.
latexmk(1) already reruns LaTeX internally, and when a document does not
converge it gives up and leaves \enquote{Rerun to get cross} in the log it
hands back to us.
Grepping for that string and relaunching latexmk(1) whenever it is present is
therefore an unbounded loop for exactly those documents---the noweb
[[longxref]] continuation lists are one, since their own line count feeds back
into the pagination they describe.
Capping the reruns ends such a build with the warning left visible in the log,
rather than hanging make(1) forever.

Whether the compilation succeeded at all is something make(1) has to be told,
and here it is not told by itself.
A recipe's exit status is the status of its \emph{last} command, and the last
command of this sequence is the rerun loop---which ends in a [[break]] as soon
as the log stops asking for another run, and [[break]] succeeds.
Separating the compilation from the loop with a [[;]], as we used to, thus
discarded latexmk(1)'s status altogether: the recipe reported success however
the compilation had gone.

That is worth more than a footnote, because a build that cannot fail cannot be
observed to have failed.
The documentation build of nytid\footnote{%
  URL: \url{https://github.com/dbosk/nytid}
} exited zero for a long time with an \enquote{Emergency stop} and a bibtex(1)
\enquote{Error 2} standing in its log, so neither its author nor its continuous
integration ever learnt that the document had stopped building.
Every kind of LaTeX breakage short of one that removes an already-built file
was hidden from make(1) the same way; a whole class of errors simply could not
be reported.

So the recipe has to look at what latexmk(1) returns.
It cannot simply fail whenever that is non-zero, though, because latexmk(1)
spends a single status---12---on two quite different situations.
One is a document with something wrong in it: LaTeX stops, and latexmk(1) ends
with \enquote{Errors, so I did not complete making targets} and gathers the
offending command's return code into a \enquote{Collected error summary}.
The other is a document that never settles: each run lays it out differently
from the one before, latexmk(1) exhausts its own rerun budget and reports
\enquote{Maximum runs of \ldots{} reached without getting stable files}.
That document is not broken.
Its last run wrote a complete file; the document merely disagrees with itself
about where a page ends.

The two deserve opposite answers, so we sort the outcomes into three.
\begin{description}
\item[The document compiled] There is nothing to say, and the recipe succeeds
  silently.
\item[The document is broken] Fail, with latexmk(1)'s own status, so that
  make(1) and whatever runs it can see it.  This is the case the paragraphs
  above are about.
\item[The document will not settle] Say so loudly, and succeed anyway.
  Whether a page break oscillates is a property of the document's layout, not
  a fault in the build: no build system can fix it, the output is complete,
  and refusing to hand it over helps nobody.
\end{description}

The motivating case is again nytid, whose documentation contains a genuine
two-cycle oscillation.
A page break moves one table-of-contents entry between two pages and drags
some thirty noweb sublabels after it, so a run from layout $A$ produces layout
$B$ and a run from $B$ produces $A$ again; latexmk(1) was once given nine runs
and still gave up.
Under a rule that failed on any non-zero status that correct document could
never be built at all---and the bounded loop above, whose whole purpose is to
carry a build past exactly this, would never get to run.

Since the status cannot tell the two apart, we ask latexmk(1) what it thinks.
We keep a copy of its output---while still letting it through to the terminal,
because these runs are long and silence during them is unhelpful---and treat
a failure as mere non-convergence only when latexmk(1) says it ran out of runs
\emph{and} reports nothing else wrong: no error summary of its own, and no
rule that its [[-use-make]] delegation ran and that then failed---which is how
a broken bibliography reaches us.
Anything else is still a failure, with latexmk(1)'s status.
<<variables>>=
LATEX?=           latexmk -dvi -use-make -8bit
PDFLATEX?=        latexmk -pdf -use-make -8bit
LATEXFLAGS?=
PREPROCESS.tex?=  ${PDFLATEX} ${LATEXFLAGS} -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR} $<
PREPROCESS.dtx?=  ${PREPROCESS.tex}
TEX_OUTDIR?=      ltxobj
# The rerun loop is bounded: latexmk already reruns internally and gives
# up when the document does not converge, leaving "Rerun to get cross"
# in the log.  An unbounded loop would then relaunch latexmk forever.
# CHECK_LATEXMK decides what each run's exit status means: silence,
# a warning, or failure.  See tex.mk.nw for why a status of its own is
# not enough to tell a broken document from an unsettled one.
COMPILE.tex?=     \
  ${RUN_PDFLATEX}; ${CHECK_LATEXMK}; \
  for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do \
    grep "Rerun to get cross" ${TEX_OUTDIR}/${<:.tex=.log} || break; \
    ${RUN_PDFLATEX}; ${CHECK_LATEXMK}; \
  done; \
  exit 0
COMPILE.dtx?=     ${COMPILE.tex}
@ Thus one would have to change the [[COMPILE.tex]] variable to change format
from PDF to DVI unless a suffix rule is used.

The two compilation variables belong to different rules.
[[COMPILE.tex]] builds the document itself, whereas [[PREPROCESS.tex]] builds
the files LaTeX writes on the way---the [[.aux]], the [[.bcf]], the [[.idx]],
the [[.nlo]]---for the rules of \cref{tex:AuxFiles}.
Both name the output directory explicitly, and for [[PREPROCESS.tex]] that is
not a matter of tidiness but of whether its rules work at all;
\cref{tex:AuxFiles} is where that argument belongs.

Each run is wrapped so that its output reaches both the terminal and a file
we can read afterwards.
The exit status has to be put aside explicitly: a pipeline reports the status
of its \emph{last} command, which here is tee(1), so latexmk(1)'s own would
otherwise be lost the moment we started keeping its output.
We also create [[TEX_OUTDIR]] first, because tee(1) opens its file before
latexmk(1) has had a chance to make the directory to put it in.
The [[$(or ...)]] around it is for the degenerate configuration where
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] has been set to nothing at all: everything else in this file
then addresses [[/]], and these two files should not be what first notices.
Falling back on the working directory leaves that configuration exactly as
broken as it was, rather than differently broken.
<<variables>>=
TEX_LATEXMKDIR?=    $(or ${TEX_OUTDIR},.)
TEX_LATEXMKLOG?=    ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).latexmklog
TEX_LATEXMKSTATUS?= ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).latexmkstatus
RUN_PDFLATEX?=      ${MKDIR} ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}; \
  { ${PDFLATEX} ${LATEXFLAGS} -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR} $<; \
    echo $$? > ${TEX_LATEXMKSTATUS}; } 2>&1 | tee ${TEX_LATEXMKLOG}
RUN_LATEX?=         ${MKDIR} ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}; \
  { ${LATEX} -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR} ${LATEXFLAGS} $<; \
    echo $$? > ${TEX_LATEXMKSTATUS}; } 2>&1 | tee ${TEX_LATEXMKLOG}
@

Then the verdict, which is the three-way policy above written out.
The two [[grep]]s spelling out \enquote{nothing else went wrong} are what
keeps this from becoming the old masking in a new disguise: a run whose
bibliography failed reports the first, and one whose [[-use-make]] delegation
ran a rule that failed reports the second.
Either fails the rule, as it should, even when latexmk(1) also ran out of
reruns---and that combination is not hypothetical, it is what a missing
prerequisite looked like when this was written.

The second [[grep]] asks for a \emph{recipe} that failed, rather than for
make(1) merely having been unhappy, because on a first pass make(1) is
routinely unhappy and rightly so.
latexmk(1) hands it the name of every file LaTeX reported missing, including
the ones LaTeX is itself about to write, and make(1) replies \enquote{No rule
to make target [[nytid.toc]]} without anything at all being wrong.
A rule that ran and came back non-zero is a different matter, and
[[*** [...] Error]] is how make(1) writes that one down.
<<variables>>=
CHECK_LATEXMK?= \
  rc=$$(cat ${TEX_LATEXMKSTATUS} 2>/dev/null); [ -n "$$rc" ] || rc=1; \
  if [ "$$rc" = 0 ]; then :; \
  elif grep -q 'without getting stable files' ${TEX_LATEXMKLOG} && \
       ! grep -q 'Collected error summary' ${TEX_LATEXMKLOG} && \
       ! grep -qE '^make(\[[0-9]+\])?: \*\*\* \[.*\] Error ' \
           ${TEX_LATEXMKLOG}; \
  then \
    echo "tex.mk: WARNING: document did not stabilize (latexmk exit $$rc);" \
      "continuing -- the output is complete, but a page break oscillates" >&2; \
  else \
    exit $$rc; \
  fi
@

%We note that we add this output directory to the search path for 
%prerequisites~\cite[see][Sect.\ 4.5.1]{GNUMake}.
%The reason for this is that we might need some out the objects as prerequisites 
%for other files.

Normally, the above is all that is needed.
However, if you need to manually build the bibliography, you can either add the
[[.bbl]] file as a prerequisite or set the following variable to a non-empty 
string.
<<variables>>=
TEX_BBL?=
@ Similarly as for the main LaTeX commands, the bibtex(1) command is controlled 
by
<<variables>>=
BIBTEX?=            bibtexu
BIBTEXFLAGS?=
BIBLIOGRAPHY.aux?=  ${BIBTEX} ${BIBTEXFLAGS} $<
@ And, in case we use biblatex, the biber(1) command is controlled by
<<variables>>=
BIBER?=             biber
BIBERFLAGS?=
BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf?=  ${BIBER} -O $@ ${BIBERFLAGS} $<
@

Similarly as for the bibliography, to enable indexing you can either manually 
add the [[.ind]] file as a prerequisite, or you can set the following variable 
to a non-empty string.
<<variables>>=
TEX_IND?=
@ The indexing-related programs are the following.
<<variables>>=
XINDY?=       texindy
XINDYFLAGS?=
COMPILE.idx?= ${XINDY} ${OUTPUT_OPTION} ${XINDYFLAGS} $<

MAKEINDEX?=   makeindex
MAKEIDXFLAGS?=
COMPILE.nlo?= ${MAKEINDEX} ${OUTPUT_OPTION} ${MAKEIDXFLAGS} -s nomencl.ist $<
@

We also provide support for PythonTeX.
This is enabled by the following variable.
<<variables>>=
TEX_PYTHONTEX?=
@ Then the required command and flags are controlled with the following 
variables.
<<variables>>=
PYTHONTEX?=       python3 $$(which pythontex)
PYTHONTEXFLAGS?=  --interpreter python:python3
@

Finally, we provide targets to easily add external classes as dependencies.
We add the phony targets
\begin{itemize}
\item[[lncs]] for Springer \ac{LNCS},
\item[[biblatex-lncs]] for the \ac{LNCS} bibliography style for the 
[[biblatex]] package,
\item[[rfc]] or [[rfc.bib]] for an up-to-date bibliography containing all 
\ac{IETF} \acp{RFC},
\item[[popets]] for the style files of the Proceedings of the Privacy Enhancing 
Technologies (PoPETS, De Gruyter Open/Sciendo).
\end{itemize}

Finally, we add a method to quickly merge bibliographies without redundancy.
This is based on the archive syntax of make(1).
This allows us to have the resulting full bibliography [[full.bib]] consist of 
the subbibliographies [[sub0.bib]] and [[sub1.bib]]:
\begin{lstlisting}
full.bib: full.bib(sub0.bib sub1.bib)
\end{lstlisting}
Or, directly as a dependency for the PDF:
\begin{lstlisting}
paper.pdf: full.bib(sub0.bib sub1.bib)
\end{lstlisting}


\section{Implementation overview}

The structure of the include file is similar to a header file in C or C++.
The include file uses the old C-style technique to prevent multiple inclusions.
<<[[tex.mk]]>>=
ifndef TEX_MK
TEX_MK=true

.NOTPARALLEL:

INCLUDE_MAKEFILES?=.
include ${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}/portability.mk

<<variables>>
<<targets for documents>>
<<targets for class and package files>>
<<targets for external classes>>
<<targets for cleaning>>

endif
@ We include [[portability.mk]] (\cref{portability.mk}) to get portable 
settings for several common utilities.
It is currently not possible to run the TeX-builds in parallel, hence the need 
for the [[.NOTPARALLEL]] target --- this runs any jobs that includes [[tex.mk]] 
  in serial.
However, this is not inherited, any recursive make invocations will run in 
parallel unless [[.NOTPARALLEL]] is specified there as well.

We will start with the targets for cleaning.
We provide two phony targets, [[clean-tex]] and [[distclean-tex]], and we 
add them as prerequisites to [[clean]] and [[distclean]], respectively.
<<targets for cleaning>>=
.PHONY: clean clean-tex
clean: clean-tex

clean-tex:
	<<clean recipe>>

.PHONY: distclean distclean-tex
distclean: distclean-tex

distclean-tex:
	<<distclean recipe>>
@ We will add to the recipes in the remainder of the chapter.
However, as latexmk(1) is set as the default in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can 
already add the following line to the cleaning recipe.
<<clean recipe>>=
-latexmk -C -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR}
[ "${TEX_OUTDIR}" -ef "$$(pwd)" ] || \
  ${RM} -R ${TEX_OUTDIR}
${RM} ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}/*.latexmklog ${TEX_LATEXMKDIR}/*.latexmkstatus
${RM} *.pytxcode
${RM} -R pythontex-files-*
@ The two [[.latexmk]] files are the copies of latexmk(1)'s output and status
kept by \cref{tex:Intro}; latexmk(1) does not know about them, so its own
[[-C]] leaves them behind.
Removing the whole of [[TEX_OUTDIR]] takes care of them wherever that is
a directory of its own, and the extra line is what catches the case where it
is the working directory instead.


\section{Targets for documents}

Now we will treat how to compile documents.
<<targets for documents>>=
<<auxillary files>>
<<bibliography files>>
<<indices files>>
<<PythonTeX files>>
<<document files>>
<<target for latexmkrc>>
@ These will be discussed in the following sections.
However, since we use latexmk(1) by default (\cref{tex:Intro}), we will discuss 
the relevant [[<<latexmkrc>>]] entries in parallel.
We supply a target to easily use our [[<<latexmkrc>>]] with latexmk(1).
<<target for latexmkrc>>=
latexmkrc:
	[ -e $@ -o "${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}" = "." ] || \
	${LN} -s ${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}/latexmkrc $@
@ We also add the corresponding line for cleaning.
<<distclean recipe>>=
[ ! -L latexmkrc ] || ${RM} latexmkrc
@

We will now discuss the different files we need latex(1) to generate.
Note that in many cases we want latex(1) to generate more files, \eg [[.toc]]
files, but we do not have to care about these here.
The reason we can ignore those files is that they do not require any external 
tool, \eg bibtex(1), to be run, these files just requires another run of 
latex(1).

\subsection{Auxillary files}
\label{tex:AuxFiles}

Many steps in compiling a LaTeX document needs the [[.aux]] file.
Thus we will first introduce a rule for creating the [[.aux]] file.
We will create it in the specified output directory.
<<auxillary files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ Then we simply do
<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>=
${MKDIR} ${TEX_OUTDIR}
${PREPROCESS.tex}
@

Making the directory and writing into it are two separate things, and the
recipe long did only the first.
[[PREPROCESS.tex]] was a bare latexmk(1) run, and a bare latexmk(1) run puts
its files beside the [[.tex]] source; the rule therefore created an empty
[[TEX_OUTDIR]], left the [[.aux]] in the working directory, and returned zero.
Nothing contradicted it.
make(1) judges a recipe by its exit status and not by what appeared on disk,
so a rule that ends successfully is a rule that has done its job.
So the rule reported success and its own target did not exist---which is the
worst of the two ways to be wrong, because the report is what the rest of the
build goes by.

Who noticed was whoever asked for the file next, one step removed from the
rule at fault.
bibtex(1) is handed [[${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux]] by the rule of
\cref{tex:Bibliographies} and answers \enquote{I couldn't open file name};
texindy(1) and makeindex(1) say as much of the [[.idx]] and the [[.nlo]].
The [[.bcf]] has no such downstream complaint to make, so there the file
simply stayed missing and the build went on as if it were not.

Telling latexmk(1) where to write is the whole fix, and it is the same
[[-output-directory]] the compilation commands of \cref{tex:Intro} already
carry.
Each of these rules then produces the target it names, which is also what
lets the rules below rely on it.

The degenerate configurations ask for nothing further.
Set [[TEX_OUTDIR]] to [[.]] and the option names the working directory, which
is where these files went anyway.
Set it to the empty string and the rules are unreachable to begin with: their
targets read [[/%.aux]] and so on, absolute patterns matching nothing we
build, just as \cref{tex:DocumentFiles} observes of [[/%.pdf]].

\subsection{Bibliographies}
\label{tex:Bibliographies}

One file that is commonly needed is the one used to create the bibliography, 
the [[.bbl]] file.
There are two ways to create this file, either using classical bibtex(1) or 
using the [[biblatex]] package and biber(1).
<<bibliography files>>=
<<bbl target for bibtex>>
<<bbl target for biber>>
@

The first approach, using bibtex(1), depends on the [[.aux]] file.
This means that we can have a target creating the desired [[.bbl]] file from 
the [[.aux]] file.
<<bbl target for bibtex>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux
	${BIBLIOGRAPHY.aux}
@

That is the whole recipe, and why it needs no second line is worth recording,
because the rule used to have one.
bibtex(1) has no output-directory option; what it has instead is the habit of
writing its [[.bbl]] and its [[.blg]] beside the [[.aux]] file it was handed.
The file we hand it is [[${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux]], so both outputs land in
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] by themselves, the [[.bbl]] under exactly the name this rule
promises.

The line that used to follow, [[${MV} $@ ${@:.bbl=.blg} ${TEX_OUTDIR}]], was
meant to carry them in from the working directory, which is where bibtex(1)
leaves them when the [[.aux]] it is given lives there.
It never could: [[$@]] is the target, and the target carries the
[[${TEX_OUTDIR}/]] prefix, so what it asked mv(1) for was to move each file
onto itself---which mv(1) declines, calling the two \enquote{the same file}.
Being the recipe's last command, that refusal failed the rule and took the
document down with it.
Repairing the names with [[$(notdir ...)]] would not help either; it would
name files that are no longer anywhere to be moved from.

The second approach uses the [[biblatex]] package~\cite{biblatex} and 
biber(1)~\cite{biber}.
They do not rely on the [[.aux]] file, instead [[biblatex]] creates a [[.bcf]] 
file.
Thus its target is exactly the same as that of the [[.aux]] file.
<<bbl target for biber>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ This [[.bcf]] file is in turn used by biber(1) to create the [[.bbl]] file.
To compile the [[.bbl]] with biber(1) and put the output files in the desired
directory, we do the following.
<<bbl target for biber>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf
	${BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf}
@

As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.bbl]] file as
a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_BBL]] is set.
<<bibliography files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_BBL},)
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl
endif
@

\subsection{Indices}

There are several time we need to work with indices, \eg when working with 
standard indices but also glossaries.
Here we provide some suffix rules to make it easier to build such indices.

Before we start, however, we will note that many of these rules are not needed 
if the [[imakeidx]] package~\cite{imakeidx} is used.
We do recommend to use this package.
Furthermore, we provide rules for the [[nomencl]] package, however, we 
recommend to use the [[glossaries]] package~\cite{glossaries} instead.
The [[glossaries]] package also has native support for xindy(1).
Although the [[glossaries]] package supports abbreviations and acronyms, we 
recommend the [[acro]] package~\cite{acro} for this instead.

The standard LaTeX index uses an [[.idx]] file, which is generated similarly as
the [[.aux]] file.
Thus we can use the same type of target.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ The actual index, which resides in a [[.ind]] file, can then be generated as 
follows.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.ind: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx
	${COMPILE.idx}
@ As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.ind]] file 
as
a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_IND]] is set.
<<indices files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_IND},)
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.ind
endif
@

For backwards compatibility, we provide the following code for the 
[[nomenclature]] package.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nlo: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>

${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nls: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nlo
	${COMPILE.nlo}
@ And now we add the corresponding code for latexmk(1).
The code is fetched from the latexmk example-files on \ac{CTAN}\footnote{%
  URL: \url{http://mirrors.ctan.org/support/latexmk/example_rcfiles/nomenclature_latexmkrc}
}.
<<[[latexmkrc]]>>=
add_cus_dep( 'nlo', 'nls', 0, 'makenlo2nls' );
sub makenlo2nls {
	system( "makeindex -s nomencl.ist -o \"$_[0].nls\" \"$_[0].nlo\"" );
}
@

\subsection{PythonTeX}

Occasionally we use PythonTeX.
We also provide a target for the required files.
However, this construction requires that we load PythonTeX as follows.
\begin{minted}{LaTeX}
\usepackage{pythontex}
\setpythontexoutputdir{.}
\setpythontexworkingdir{..}
\end{minted}

The code is as follows.
As with the document files of \cref{tex:DocumentFiles}, we let the [[.pytxmcr]]
file be asked for under both its plain name and its name inside
[[TEX_OUTDIR]]; and for the reason explained there, that has to be written as
one rule per name instead of one rule listing both.
<<PythonTeX files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxcode: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux
	cd $(dir $@) && ${PYTHONTEX} ${PYTHONTEXFLAGS} $(basename $(notdir $@))
%.pytxmcr:: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxcode
	<<run PythonTeX in the output directory>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxmcr:: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxcode
	<<run PythonTeX in the output directory>>
@

Both rules keep the double colon they had as a pair.
On a pattern rule the double colon does not mean what it means on an ordinary
one: it marks the rule \emph{terminal}, so make(1) will not build the
[[.pytxcode]] prerequisite through another implicit rule but requires it to
exist already~\cite[Sect.\ 10.5.5]{GNUMake}.
That is deliberate here---latexmk(1) writes the [[.pytxcode]] as a side effect
of running LaTeX---so splitting the targets must not quietly drop it.

PythonTeX writes its output next to the [[.pytxcode]] file, that is inside
[[TEX_OUTDIR]], whichever of the two names make(1) was asked for.
Naming the recipe once and referring to it from both rules keeps them
identical, so there is no second copy to drift.
<<run PythonTeX in the output directory>>=
cd ${TEX_OUTDIR} && ${PYTHONTEX} ${PYTHONTEXFLAGS} $(basename $(notdir $@))
@

As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.pytxmcr]] 
file as a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_PYTHONTEX]] is set.
<<bibliography files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_PYTHONTEX},)
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxmcr
endif
@

If we use latexmk(1), then we must also add instructions for this in 
[[<<latexmkrc>>]].
The following code is fetched from the latexmk example-files on 
\ac{CTAN}\footnote{%
  URL: \url{http://mirrors.ctan.org/support/latexmk/example_rcfiles/pythontex-latexmkrc}
}.
<<[[latexmkrc]]>>=
#  This version has a fudge on the latex and pdflatex commands that
#  allows the pythontex custom dependency to work even when $out_dir
#  is used to set the output directory.  Without the fudge (done by
#  trickery symbolic links) the custom dependency for using pythontex
#  will not be detected.

add_cus_dep('pytxcode', 'pytxmcr', 0, 'pythontex');
sub pythontex {
    # This subroutine is a fudge, because it from latexmk's point of
    # view, it makes the main .tex file depend on the .pytxcode file.
    # But it doesn't actually make the .tex file, but is used for its
    # side effects in creating other files.  The dependence is a way
    # of triggering the rule to be run whenever the .pytxcode file
    # changes, and to do this before running latex/pdflatex again.
    return system("pythontex3 --verbose \"$_[0]\"");
}

$pdflatex = 'internal mylatex %R %Z pdflatex %O %S';
$latex = 'internal mylatex %R %Z latex %O %S';
sub mylatex {
   my $root = shift;
   my $dir_string = shift;
   my $code = "$root.pytxcode";
   my $result = "pythontex-files-$root";
   if ($dir_string) {
      warn "mylatex: Making symlinks to fool cus_dep creation\n";
      unlink $code;
      if (-l $result) {
          unlink $result;
      }
      elsif (-d $result) {
         unlink glob "$result/*";
         rmdir $result;
      }
      symlink $dir_string.$code, $code;
      if ( ! -e $dir_string.$result ) { mkdir $dir_string.$result; }
      symlink $dir_string.$result, $result;
   }
   else {
      foreach ($code, $result) { if (-l) { unlink; } }
   }
   return system @_;
}
@

\subsection{Document files}
\label{tex:DocumentFiles}

Now that we have all prerequisite files, we can actually compile the document.
For simplicity we add file in both the current working directory and the
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] as targets.
The reason for this is that it makes the makefile easier to write, usually we
prefer writing just the [[.pdf]] file --- and not the path to the [[.pdf]] in
the [[TEX_OUTDIR]] directory.
And for the same reason, the rule for the plain name links it to the compiled
file afterwards --- this allows make(1) to track modification times correctly.
(We need a symbolic link, rather than a hard link, because upon recompilation,
the file in [[TEX_OUTDIR]] is unlinked, whereas the other is not.)

These two names are \emph{alternatives}: each is a way of asking for the same
document, and one compilation satisfies whichever one was asked for.
That is not what a pattern rule with two targets means to make(1).
Unlike an ordinary rule, a pattern rule listing several targets does not stand
for several independent rules; make(1) reads it as a single recipe that
produces \emph{all} of them~\cite[Sect.\ 10.5.1]{GNUMake}.
Since GNU Make 4.4 that reading is said out loud, as a \enquote{pattern recipe
did not update peer target} warning for every listed target the recipe did not
happen to write---and our recipe writes one of the two names and links the
other, so the claim was wrong to begin with.

We therefore give each name its own single-target rule.
The rules make(1) ends up with are the same; only the false claim that one run
produces both goes away.
The split leaves the two patterns distinct whenever [[TEX_OUTDIR]] names a
separate directory, which is the default ([[ltxobj]]): asking for [[doc.pdf]]
selects the first rule, asking for [[ltxobj/doc.pdf]] the second, and both
compile the document the same way.
Setting [[TEX_OUTDIR]] to [[.]] makes the two patterns coincide instead, since
make(1) reads [[./%.pdf]] and [[%.pdf]] as one name; of two pattern rules with
the same target and prerequisite patterns it keeps the later, and [[make -p]]
duly reports a single [[%.pdf: %.tex]] carrying the second rule's recipe.
That is the one to keep.
When the output directory is the working directory, the compilation has
already written the file under the plain name, and the rule that links
nothing is exactly the rule such a build wants.
The order within each pair is thus load-bearing in that one configuration,
where before the split it was not.
And [[TEX_OUTDIR]] is never simply unset, because the [[?=]] assignment above
gives it a default; were it set to the empty string, the second target would
read [[/%.pdf]], an absolute pattern matching nothing we build, both before
and after this change.
<<document files>>=
%.pdf: %.tex
	<<compile PDF>>
	<<link the plain name to the output directory>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: %.tex
	<<compile PDF>>

%.dvi: %.tex
	<<compile DVI>>
	<<link the plain name to the output directory>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.dvi: %.tex
	<<compile DVI>>
@

The compilation is written once and referred to from both rules of a pair, so
the work they genuinely have in common cannot drift apart.
<<compile PDF>>=
${COMPILE.tex}
@

What the two rules do \emph{not} share is the link, and that is the whole
point of it.
The link exists to give the plain name to a file the compilation wrote inside
[[TEX_OUTDIR]]; a rule whose target already carries that prefix has been
handed the file under the name it was asked for, and has nothing left to
link.
Both rules used to run [[-${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@]] all the same, which is
right for [[doc.pdf]] and nonsense for [[ltxobj/doc.pdf]]: with the prefix
already in [[$@]] it expands to
\begin{lstlisting}
ln -sf ltxobj/ltxobj/doc.pdf ltxobj/doc.pdf
\end{lstlisting}
and replaces the PDF that had just been compiled with a symbolic link to
a path that does not exist.
The leading [[-]] then swallowed whatever ln(1) had to say, so make(1)
reported success over a document that was no longer there.
The [[.dtx]] rules of \cref{tex:ClassFiles} did the same.

The plain name has a version of the same accident, in the configuration where
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] is the empty string: there [[${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@]] reads
[[/doc.pdf]], and linking the one real file away to it is destructive in
exactly the same way.
Setting [[TEX_OUTDIR]] to [[.]] gets no further either, but there ln(1) saves
us---it declines to link a file to itself, calling the two \enquote{the same
file}---at the price of an error message on every build.

Both are the same question asked too late: whether the two names are two
files at all.
We ask it first instead, and of the directories rather than the names, since
that is where the answer comes from.
<<link the plain name to the output directory>>=
-[ "$(or ${TEX_OUTDIR},.)" -ef . ] || ${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@
@

The test is the one [[clean-tex]] already uses to decide whether
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] is a directory of its own.
[[-ef]] compares the files behind the names instead of the names themselves,
so a [[TEX_OUTDIR]] of [[./ltxobj]], or an absolute path naming the working
directory, is recognised as well as a plain [[.]]; and the [[$(or ...)]]
covers the empty string, where the directory latexmk(1) writes into is the
working directory.
When the two do name one directory, the compilation has already written the
file under the plain name and the recipe is done.
The [[-]] stays: a link that cannot be made is a nuisance, not a reason to
throw away a document that compiled.

The compilation step for DVI is the usual.
We compile once, then we recompile as long as the log file tells us to---and,
for the reason given in \cref{tex:Intro}, at most five times.
A DVI document can fail to settle exactly as a PDF one can, so this is the
same shape as [[COMPILE.tex]], down to the same verdict after every run; only
the engine differs.
The whole thing is one recipe line rather than several because [[exit]] ends
the shell make(1) started for the line it appears in, and the verdict has to
be able to end the run wherever it is reached.
<<compile DVI>>=
${RUN_LATEX}; ${CHECK_LATEXMK}; \
for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do \
  grep "Rerun to get cross" ${TEX_OUTDIR}/${<:.tex=.log} || break; \
  ${RUN_LATEX}; ${CHECK_LATEXMK}; \
done; \
exit 0
@


\section{Targets for merging bibliographies}

To merge bibliographies we will use [[bibtool]] and make's archive rules.
In particular, we extend the general rules we defined in [[portability.mk]].
<<variables>>=
BIBTOOL?=     bibtool
BIBTOOLFLAGS?=--preserve.key.case=on --print.deleted.entries=off -s -d -r biblatex
ARCHIVE.bib?= ${CAT} $(if $(wildcard $@),$@) $% | \
  ${BIBTOOL} ${BIBTOOLFLAGS} -o $@
@


\section{Targets for class and package files}
\label{tex:ClassFiles}

There are two parts concerning class and package files.
<<targets for class and package files>>=
<<compile sty and cls files>>
<<compile class and package documentation>>
@ These are very similar to what we have done above, especially the 
documentation.

Compiling a class or package from DocTeX source is easier than compiling 
a document.
We can normally create the [[.sty]] and [[.cls]] files by running latex(1) on 
the [[.ins]] file.
<<compile sty and cls files>>=
%.cls %.sty: %.ins
	${LATEX} $<
@

We can then compile the documentation similarly to how we compile normal
documents---similarly enough that these rules inherited the flaw of
\cref{tex:DocumentFiles} along with the shape.
Here too each output answers to two names, the plain one and the one inside
[[TEX_OUTDIR]], and listing both as targets of one pattern rule again claims
that a single run produces both.
It does not: the run writes the name inside [[TEX_OUTDIR]] and links the
other, so make(1) is left to complain about whichever of the two that
particular invocation did not touch.
We therefore give each name its own rule here as well.

Unlike the [[.pytxmcr]] rules earlier, these carry a single colon, so there is
no terminal-rule property that the split has to preserve; the targets are all
that come apart.
The [[.dvi]] pair needs no recipe of its own either---compiling a class or
package to DVI is the same work as compiling a document---so it reuses the one
named in \cref{tex:DocumentFiles}.
<<compile class and package documentation>>=
%.pdf: %.dtx
	<<compile package PDF>>
	<<link the plain name to the output directory>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: %.dtx
	<<compile package PDF>>

%.dvi: %.dtx
	<<compile DVI>>
	<<link the plain name to the output directory>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.dvi: %.dtx
	<<compile DVI>>
@

Only the PDF recipe is genuinely its own, and only because DocTeX gets
compiled through [[COMPILE.dtx]] rather than [[COMPILE.tex]].
The link follows the same division as in \cref{tex:DocumentFiles}, for the
same reason: only the rule whose target lacks the [[TEX_OUTDIR]] prefix has
a name left to give the file.
<<compile package PDF>>=
${COMPILE.dtx}
@ However, we must tell make(1) how to make a [[.bbl]] etc.\ from [[.dtx]].
These three files are alternatives in much the same sense as the two names of
\cref{tex:DocumentFiles}: which of them a build wants follows from what the
class loads, and one preprocessing run writes whichever of them that
particular document has.
Listed as the targets of a single pattern rule they instead claim that every
run writes all three, and GNU Make 4.4 answers with a \enquote{pattern recipe
did not update peer target} for each one it did not.
The rule was quiet about that for as long as its recipe wrote none of the
three; now that the run goes where the targets are, it writes one, and make(1)
starts pointing at the other two.
So here too we give each name a rule of its own.
<<compile class and package documentation>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux: %.dtx
	<<create output directory and preprocess DocTeX>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf: %.dtx
	<<create output directory and preprocess DocTeX>>
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx: %.dtx
	<<create output directory and preprocess DocTeX>>
@

The recipe is the one of \cref{tex:AuxFiles} with [[PREPROCESS.dtx]] in the
place of [[PREPROCESS.tex]], so that a project compiling its DocTeX sources
with something of its own keeps that choice in these rules as well.
Naming it once is what keeps the three copies from drifting apart.
<<create output directory and preprocess DocTeX>>=
${MKDIR} ${TEX_OUTDIR}
${PREPROCESS.dtx}
@


\section{External classes and packages}

Occasionally, we are required to use document classes that are not in 
\ac{CTAN}.
Here we provide targets for some such classes and packages.
<<targets for external classes>>=
<<a general downloader>>
<<Springer LNCS>>
<<biblatex LNCS style>>
<<the RFC bibliography>>
<<PoPETS>>
@

We will now construct a general downloader, then we will use this downloader to
write targets for the external classes we are interested in.
In general, what we want this function to do is to download an archive or 
repository ([[TEX_EXT_SRC]]), extract the files we are interested in 
([[TEX_EXT_FILES]]) to the destination directory ([[TEX_EXT_DIR]]), and 
finally, create symbolic links to those files from the current working 
directory.
<<a general downloader>>=
define download_archive
<<targets for symlinks>>
<<targets for desired files>>
<<targets for archive>>
<<target for cleaning>>
endef
define download_repo
<<targets for symlinks>>
<<targets for desired files>>
<<targets for repo>>
<<target for cleaning>>
endef
@

The first thing we want to do is to generate targets and recipes for how to 
create the symbolic links, assuming that the target files already exists.
First we set up a dependency between the files we are interested in and where 
that file is actually located.
Then we create a recipe which will create a symbolic link between them.
We use the [[notdir]] function~\cite[Sect.\ 8.3]{GNUMake} to remove any 
directory-part since we want to create the symbolic link in the current working
directory.
<<targets for symlinks>>=
$(foreach file,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)},\
  $(eval $(notdir ${file}): ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${file}))
$(notdir ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${LN} $$^ $$@
@ Note that we need the [[eval]] command above to evaluate the rule for each 
file, otherwise we would get \emph{one line} with many colons --- which is not 
valid syntax for make(1).
To make it easier to add these files as prerequisites to a target, we also 
provide the following phony target.
<<targets for symlinks>>=
.PHONY: $(1)
$(1): $(notdir ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)})
@

Now we need something to trigger the download of the archive or the repository.
One way to do this is to add a prerequisite for the files from the archive or 
repository.
<<targets for desired files>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}): \
  ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}
@ Now we turn to the recipe.
In the case of an archive, we must extract the desired files.
We let the variable [[TEX_EXT_EXTRACT]] contain the extraction command.
<<targets for archive>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-$(1)}
@ For a repository we can simply copy the file or create a link.
We prefer the latter.
<<targets for repo>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${LN} ${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}/$${@:${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/%=%} $$@
@

The file [[TEX_EXT_SRC]] can be either an archive or a repository.
We let [[TEX_EXT_URL]] be the \ac{URL} to fetch it from in both cases.
In the case of an archive we do the following.
<<targets for archive>>=
${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}:
	${MKDIR} ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}
	${CURL} -o $$@ ${TEX_EXT_URL-$(1)}
@ In the case of a repository, we simply clone it.
<<targets for repo>>=
${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}:
	git clone ${TEX_EXT_URL-$(1)} $$@
@ We note that the directory of the repo should be an order-only 
prerequisite~\cite[see][Sect.\ 4.3]{GNUMake} for the files inside.
Unfortunately this is not the case at the moment.

Finally, we must also do some cleaning.
<<target for cleaning>>=
.PHONY: distclean clean-$(1)
distclean: clean-$(1)
clean-$(1):
	${RM} ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}
	[ "${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}" = "." ] && ${RM} ${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)} \
	  || ${RM} -R ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}
@

\subsection{Springer LNCS}

Springer's \ac{LNCS} series is used for the proceedings of many 
conferences.
The style files are available on Springer's web site, but unfortunately not 
under any permissive license\footnote{%
  It would be most desirable that they were made available in \ac{CTAN} under 
  an open license.
}.
So, we must, each and every one of us, connect to Springer's server and 
download our own copy.
This is what we automate here.

We use the downloader described above.
<<Springer LNCS>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-lncs?=  llncs.cls sprmindx.sty splncs03.bst aliascnt.sty remreset.sty
TEX_EXT_DIR-lncs?=    lncs
TEX_EXT_SRC-lncs?=    llncs2e.zip
TEX_EXT_URL-lncs?=    https://resource-cms.springernature.com/springer-cms/rest/v1/content/19238648/data/v1
TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-lncs?=${UNZIP} -u $$< -d ${TEX_EXT_DIR-lncs}

$(eval $(call download_archive,lncs))
@

We also want to add backwards compatibility for when we used [[llncs]] instead 
of just [[lncs]].
<<Springer LNCS>>=
.PHONY: llncs
llncs: lncs
@

\subsection{LNCS style for \texttt{biblatex}}

There is also \iac{LNCS} style for the [[biblatex]] package available on 
GitHub.
Since it is available on GitHub, we recommend adding it as a Git submodule.
I.e.\ run the following command.
\begin{lstlisting}
git submodule add https://github.com/neapel/biblatex-lncs.git
\end{lstlisting}
This will add a directory [[biblatex-lncs]] to the current directory.

If we do not add it as a submodule we can use the downloader above.
<<biblatex LNCS style>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-biblatex-lncs?= lncs.bbx lncs.cbx lncs.dbx
TEX_EXT_DIR-biblatex-lncs?=   lncs
TEX_EXT_SRC-biblatex-lncs?=   biblatex-lncs
TEX_EXT_URL-biblatex-lncs?=   https://github.com/NorwegianRockCat/biblatex-lncs.git

$(eval $(call download_repo,biblatex-lncs))
@

\subsection{The RFC bibliography}

Occasionally we want to cite \ac{IETF} \acp{RFC}.
Fortunately, Roland Bless of Karlsruher Institute of Technology provides an 
up-to-date bibliography file for all \acp{RFC}, so we will use that one.
This is a single file, so we do not need to use the downloader.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
rfc.bib:
	<<download rfc.bib>>
	<<change misc to techreport>>

${TEXMF}/tex/latex/rfc.bib:
	mkdir -p ${TEXMF}/tex/latex/
	<<download rfc.bib>>
	<<change misc to techreport>>
@

We will use curl(1) to download a compressed version from Bless' site.
We let curl(1) output the contents to standard out and pipe it to the 
uncompress(1) utility and, finally, redirect the result to the target file.
<<download rfc.bib>>=
${CURL} -o - http://tm.uka.de/~bless/rfc.bib.gz 2>/dev/null \
  | ${UNCOMPRESS.gz} - > $@ ; \
@

According to \ac{IETF}~\cite[Sect.\ 5.2]{IETFCitingRFCs} the \acp{RFC} should 
be cited as the [[techreport]] BibTeX type.
<<change misc to techreport>>=
${SED} -i "s/@misc/@techreport/" $@
@

We also provide a phony target for these two files.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
.PHONY: rfc
rfc: rfc.bib ${TEXMF}/tex/latex/rfc.bib
@

Finally, we provide a phony cleaning for cleaning.
The target is named [[clean-rfc]] and is added as a prerequisite for 
[[distclean]] --- this way its recipe will not interfere with any cleaning 
recipe written by the user.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
.PHONY: distclean clean-rfc
distclean: clean-rfc
clean-rfc:
	${RM} rfc.bib
@

\subsection{Proceedings of the Privacy Enhancing Technologies Symposium}

We would also like to be able to use the PoPETS format.
<<PoPETS>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-popets?=by-nc-nd.pdf sciendo-logo.pdf dgruyter_NEW.sty
TEX_EXT_URL-popets?=https://petsymposium.org/files/popets.zip
TEX_EXT_DIR-popets?=popets
TEX_EXT_SRC-popets?=popets.zip
TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-popets?=${UNZIP} -p $$< popets/$$(notdir $$@) > $$@

$(eval $(call download_archive,popets))
@
